  "response": "Adjudicating an abrupt regional temperature anomaly between internal variability and forced change begins with fixing the observational pattern itself. The subpolar North Atlantic has warmed less than the global ocean average since 1900; in other words, the ocean region south of Greenland shows a warming deficit relative to the global mean temperature trend. Sea surface temperature in the cold blob region rose faster than the global mean over the twentieth century, and an excess of regional warming cannot be squared with a deficit over the same waters south of Greenland: the two descriptions cannot both hold.\n\nThe attribution problem is then posed over a system in which at least one of two contributions operates, and perhaps both: internal variability of ocean circulation contributes to the cold anomaly in the subpolar North Atlantic, and anthropogenic forcing contributes to that anomaly as well. The formal machinery for separating them is pattern-based. Optimal fingerprinting compares observed spatial patterns of temperature change with model-simulated responses to external forcing. CMIP6 models forced with rising greenhouse gas concentrations simulate a warming hole in the North Atlantic, which is strong evidence that anthropogenic forcing does contribute to the subpolar cold anomaly.\n\nMeasurements of the ice sheet and of regional surface salinity enter the assessment at this point. The IMBIE assessment estimated that Greenland lost roughly 3,900 gigatonnes of ice between 1992 and 2018, and that mass loss has resulted in meltwater from the Greenland Ice Sheet freshening the surface waters of the subpolar North Atlantic. That meltwater freshening of the subpolar surface has in turn caused exactly the following: reduced deep convection in the Labrador Sea weakens northward ocean heat transport.\n\nQuantifying the circulation itself demands sustained instrumentation. The RAPID-MOCHA mooring array monitors the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation at 26.5\u00b0N. The RAPID array was installed in 1984 along the 45\u00b0N transect, a siting that cannot be reconciled with a monitoring line at 26.5\u00b0N; one or the other locates the moorings, not both. Whereas moored instruments gauge the modern overturning directly, the centuries preceding them are reconstructed from the seafloor. Sortable silt grain-size records serve as paleoceanographic proxies for past deep current strength, and such grain-size evidence indicates an AMOC weakening of about 15 percent since the mid-twentieth century, as Caesar and colleagues estimated. A decline of that magnitude is evidence that northward ocean heat transport has slackened as deep convection in the Labrador Sea has diminished. Adjudication of the cold blob thus rests on the joint verdict of fingerprint analysis, measured circulation and sedimentary reconstruction.",
